readings.c2s1:
¶

'''unbefool''' (un-bẹ̄-föl′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''befool.''] '''1.''' To change from a foolish nature; restore from the state or condition of a fool. ''South'', Sermons, VII. viii. —'''2.''' To open the eyes of to a sense of folly. —'''3.''' To undeceive. [Rare in all uses.]

'''unbefriend''' (un-bẹ̄-frend′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''befriend.''] To fail to befriend; deprive of friendly support. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|And will not ''unbefriend'' the enterprising any more than the timid. {{right|''The American'', XXIX. 104.}}}}

'''unbefriended''' (un-bẹ̄-fren′ded), ''p. a.'' Not befriended; not supported by friends; having no friendly aid.
{{smaller block|<poem>Alas for Love!
And Truth who wanderest lone and ''unbefriended.''</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Hellas.}}}}

'''unbeget''' (un-bẹ̄-get′), ''v. t.'' To undo the begetting of. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Wishes each minute he could ''unbeget'' those rebel sons. {{right|''Dryden'', Aurengzebe, i. 1.}}}}

'''unbeginning''' (un-bẹ̄-gin′ing), ''a.'' Having no beginning.
{{smaller block|An ''unbeginning'', midless, endless ball. {{right|''Sylvester.''}}}}

'''unbegot''' (un-bẹ̄-got′), ''a.'' Unbegotten.
{{smaller block|<poem>Your children yet unborn and ''unbegot.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., iii. 3. 88.}}}}

'''unbegotten''' (un-bẹ̄-got′n), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not yet generated; not yet begotten. —'''2.''' Having never been generated; self-existent.
{{smaller block|The eternal, ''unbegotten'', and immutable God.}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''T. Goodwin'', Works, III. 372.}}}}
{{smaller block|“I was there,” resumed Mrs. Cluppins, “''unbeknown'' to Mrs. Bardell.”
{{right|''Dickens'', Pickwick, xxxiv.}}}}

'''unbeknownst''' (un-bē-nōnst′), ''a.'' Same as ''unbeknown''. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|So by & by I creep up softly to my own old little room, not to disturb their pleasure, & ''unbeknownst'' to most.
{{right|''E. S. Phelps'', Sealed Orders.}}}}

'''unbelief''' (un-bē-lēf′), ''n.'' '''1.''' Incredulity; the withholding of belief; disbelief; especially, disbelief of divine revelation.
{{smaller block|<poem>[Truth] shines in all who do not shut it out
By dungeon doors of ''unbelief'' and doubt.</poem>
{{right|''Abraham Coles'', The Evangel, p. 181.}}}}
{{smaller block|Belief consists in accepting the affirmations of the soul; ''unbelief'', in denying them.
{{right|''Emerson'', Montaigne.}}}}
'''2.''' Disbelief of the truth of the gospel; distrust of God’s promises and faithfulness, etc. Mat. xiii. 58; Mark vi. 6; Heb. iii. 12.='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|Distrust.—'''1''' and '''2.''' ''Disbelief'', ''Unbelief'' (see ''disbelief''), skepticism.}}

'''unbeliefful†''' (un-bē-lēf′fu̇l), ''a.'' [ME. ''unbeleveful''; < ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''beliefful''.] Full of unbelief; unbelieving.
{{smaller block|He that is ''unbeleveful'' to the sone, schal not se euerlastinge lyf.
{{right|''Wyclif'', John iii. 36.}}}}

'''unbelieffulness†''' (un-bē-lēf′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' [ME. ''unbilevefulness''; < ''unbeliefful'' + ''-ness''.] Unbelief; want of faith.
{{smaller block|And anon the fadir of the child criynge with teeris seide: Lord, I beleve, help thou myn ''vnbilevefulness''.}}

